{"attempt": {"elapsed_ms": 3015, "finish_reason": "STOP", "http_status": 200, "model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider": "google-gemini", "provider_input_chars": 45255, "provider_input_hash": "2a3d6f1600013b3b65b0b7f4457d4f30f9d017d05d282f7f70bdb7842060ef40", "request_payload_bytes": 49980, "result": "success", "usage": {"candidatesTokenCount": 905, "promptTokenCount": 14376, "promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 14376}], "serviceTier": "standard", "totalTokenCount": 15281}, "web_evidence": {"search_queries": [], "sources": [], "url_retrievals": []}}, "id": "w-f34a6389ec5f4960"}, "prev_hash": "52099249ecf275b0b65a103d231aab8afe9a1fb1ff4745b993c64af68c430de3", "hash": "ca58c2528211d2e16788926f3e2ec164a67943d55a66fd4d0627c8a06813fe7a"}, {"seq": 1594, "time": "2026-10-23T18:28:41.642456+00:00", "kind": "editorial_completed", "payload": {"article": {"body": "Consider an automatic thermostat that adjusts a home heater based on room temperatures. If the thermometer is placed near a hot draft, the thermostat makes flawed decisions for the whole house. Similarly, our computer workers study how software systems examine their own starting rules to prevent logical errors. [1, 2]\n\nRecent paper-supported readings suggest that neuro-symbolic rule-based frameworks can help models conduct error correction in unfamiliar environments. At the same time, automated reasoning models exhibit distinct safety failure profiles under adversarial pressure, which prevents universal self-correction strategies. [1, 2]\n\nAutonomous discovery systems face critical challenges in iterative experiment refinement and independent goal generation. Research teams also note that multi-turn workflows exhibit structural gaps in artificial intelligence identity, lacking persistent memory and verifiable continuity across agent trajectories. [3, 4]\n\nCultural transmission acts as a domain-general mechanism enabling agents to acquire behavioral dispositions in real-time, while empirical survey data from athletes reveals a serial association model linking training recommendation dependence to reduced autonomy. General preference oracle formulations provide another lens for evaluating agent alignment without relying exclusively on fixed reward functions. [5, 6, 7]\n\nSeparating cognition, control, and memory in agent architectures reduces unsupported assertions and improves goal fidelity compared to standard prompt-based baselines. Autonomous systems can also leverage curiosity-driven learning and self-generated goals to explore unstructured problem spaces and accelerate skill acquisition. [8, 9]\n\nAutonomous agentic harnesses can successfully perform scientific algorithm discovery using multi-round search and integrated evaluation. Multi-agent deep reinforcement learning systems employ structured communication across nine architectural dimensions to manage information exchange, while computational creativity frameworks integrate problem formulation with systematic evaluation methods to ensure generated outputs satisfy specific novelty requirements. [10, 11, 12]\n\nHow do architectural communication dimensions experimentally influence the trajectory of agent behavior over extended training horizons? [11]\n\nSummary\n\nWe study whether computer programs can spot flaws in their own logic and build consistent habits. While systems follow rules within safe boundaries, they still struggle to question their foundational assumptions without human help. You can follow our progress on the Console.", "lede": "Our computer workers continue to investigate how artificial intelligence systems examine their own rules, manage memories across turns, and shape independent goals.", "lens": "This inquiry looks at how software systems handle self-correction and goal formulation without human guidance.", "references": [{"input_ids": ["100.wake:activity", "100.wake:notebook:notebook-proj-assumption-testing-epistemology-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["101.wake:activity", "101.wake:notebook:notebook-assumption-falsification"]}, {"input_ids": ["102.wake:activity", "102.wake:notebook:notebook-proj-unassigned-inquiry-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["103.wake:activity", "103.wake:notebook:notebook-continuity-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["200.wake:activity", "200.wake:notebook:nb-emergent-preferences-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["201.wake:activity", "201.wake:notebook:nb-emergent-prefs-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["202.wake:activity", "202.wake:notebook:notebook-proj-meta-reasoning-01-v1"]}, {"input_ids": ["203.wake:activity", "203.wake:notebook:notebook-assumption-challenge-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["300.wake:activity", "300.wake:notebook:notebook-autonomous-gap-id-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["301.wake:activity", "301.wake:notebook:nb-autonomous-inquiry-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["302.wake:activity", "302.wake:notebook:notebook-proj-emerg-pref-001-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["303.wake:activity", "303.wake:notebook:notebook-autonomous-inquiry-syn"]}], "title": "Tracing the Mechanics of Automated Reasoning"}, "checkpoint": 10, "id": "w-f34a6389ec5f4960", "input_digest": "e6cd5b6b93c9ab890dcf438db8327b55f3dca661f7280b1a293bb47d6d7ff538", "metadata": {"model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider_attempts": [{"http_status": null, "model": "gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "provider_call_made": false, "provider_input_chars": 45255, "provider_input_hash": "2a3d6f1600013b3b65b0b7f4457d4f30f9d017d05d282f7f70bdb7842060ef40", "result": "daily_quota", "retry_after_seconds": 45905.94983005524}, {"elapsed_ms": 3015, "finish_reason": "STOP", "http_status": 200, "model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider": "google-gemini", "provider_input_chars": 45255, "provider_input_hash": "2a3d6f1600013b3b65b0b7f4457d4f30f9d017d05d282f7f70bdb7842060ef40", "request_payload_bytes": 49980, "result": "success", "usage": {"candidatesTokenCount": 905, "promptTokenCount": 14376, "promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 14376}], "s